---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc978.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "1. Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "2. Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "3. Timeshare Interval Sales — ASC 606 Revenue Recognition" },
        { id: "s4", label: "4. Inventory Costing — the Relative Sales Value Method" },
        { id: "s5", label: "5. Uncollectible Accounts — the Variable Consideration Constraint" },
        { id: "s6", label: "6. Worked Example — a Timeshare Interval Sale Recognition" },
        { id: "s7", label: "7. Worked Example — Relative Sales Value Inventory Costing" },
        { id: "s8", label: "8. Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "9. Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "10. ASC 978 vs IFRS — Comparison" },
        { id: "s11", label: "11. Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "12. Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "13. Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Selling fractional intervals, with historically elevated default risk</div><div class="principle">TIMESHARE (vacation ownership interval) sales involve selling FRACTIONAL usage rights in a resort unit — typically financed and, like retail land sales (ASC 976), historically associated with notably HIGH cancellation and DEFAULT rates. ASC 978 addresses REVENUE recognition (now under ASC 606's general framework) and the DISTINCTIVE inventory COSTING challenge of allocating shared project costs across MANY individual, differently-priced timeshare intervals.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Developers and sellers of vacation ownership intervals</div><div class="principle">ASC 978 applies to entities developing and selling TIMESHARE (vacation ownership) intervals — fractional rights to use a resort property for a specified period each year.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Timeshare Interval Sales — ASC 606 Revenue Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Control transfer, with careful collectibility assessment</div><div class="principle">Timeshare interval sale revenue is recognised under ASC 606's GENERAL model — upon transfer of CONTROL of the interval to the buyer, PROVIDED collection of consideration is deemed PROBABLE. Given the industry's historically ELEVATED default/cancellation rates, careful assessment of COLLECTIBILITY (as part of the general contract existence criteria) and appropriate constraint of VARIABLE CONSIDERATION are PARTICULARLY significant in practice for this industry.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Inventory Costing — the Relative Sales Value Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Allocating shared resort development costs across many intervals</div><div class="principle">A SINGLE resort property is typically divided into MANY individual timeshare INTERVALS (e.g. 52 weekly intervals per unit, across potentially dozens of units), each sold at DIFFERENT times and DIFFERENT prices (e.g. a summer week commands a higher price than an off-season week). Total resort development costs are ALLOCATED to individual intervals using the RELATIVE SALES VALUE method (the SAME general approach described in the ASC 970 poster) — allocating cost proportionately based on each interval's estimated relative sales value.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Uncollectible Accounts — the Variable Consideration Constraint</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Constraining the transaction price given historically high default rates</div><div class="principle">Given the timeshare industry's historically SIGNIFICANT rates of buyer default/rescission, sellers must carefully apply ASC 606's VARIABLE CONSIDERATION constraint — recognising revenue only for the amount HIGHLY PROBABLE not to result in a significant future REVERSAL, drawing on the entity's OWN historical default/cancellation experience for similar buyer profiles and interval types.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Worked Example — a Timeshare Interval Sale Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Constraining revenue for expected defaults</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Selling a summer week interval</div><div class="wk-body">A developer sells a summer-week timeshare interval for $22,000, financed through the developer over 5 years. Based on historical experience with similar buyer profiles, the developer estimates a 12% eventual default rate on such financed sales.</div><div class="wk-answer">Applying the variable consideration constraint, the developer recognises revenue of $22,000 × (1 − 12%) = <strong>$19,360</strong> upon transfer of control of the interval — CONSTRAINING the transaction price to the amount highly probable of NOT being reversed, given the historically elevated default risk in this specific market.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Relative Sales Value Inventory Costing</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Allocating shared resort costs across intervals</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Allocating cost to a premium summer interval</div><div class="wk-body">A resort's total development cost is $10,000,000, with a TOTAL estimated sales value across ALL intervals of $15,000,000. A specific premium summer-week interval has an estimated sales value of $30,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">Allocated cost for this interval = $10,000,000 × ($30,000 ÷ $15,000,000) = $10,000,000 × 0.2% = <strong>$20,000</strong> — the interval's PROPORTIONATE share of total resort development cost, based on its relative SALES value compared to the total pool of all intervals.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 978 requires (in addition to general ASC 606 disclosures)</div><div class="check-list"><span>Inventory costing methodology used for allocating resort development costs to intervals</span><span>Historical default/uncollectible rates used to constrain variable consideration</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether collection is probable, given historically elevated default rates</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Expected default/cancellation rates used to constrain variable consideration</span><span>Relative sales values of individual intervals within a resort project</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>ASC 978 vs IFRS — Comparison</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 978 vs general IFRS 15 principles</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 978)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRS 15)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Revenue recognition model</span><span>General ASC 606 control transfer and variable consideration constraint</span><span>Same general IFRS 15 model — converged</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Inventory cost allocation</span><span>Relative sales value method, described</span><span>No dedicated named method, but general cost allocation principles achieve similar outcomes</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">Since timeshare revenue now follows the SAME general ASC 606/IFRS 15 framework as retail land sales, this is another substantially CONVERGED specialised real estate Topic.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 978 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Timeshare interval sale revenue</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised upon control transfer, constrained for expected defaults</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Interval cost of sales</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Allocated using the relative sales value method</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general control-transfer and variable consideration constraint framework</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 970</span><span>Real Estate—General — supplies the general relative sales value cost allocation concept applied here</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 976</span><span>Real Estate—Retail Land — a closely related, similarly-structured specialised real estate sale Topic</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Timeshare interval</div><div class="glossary-def">A fractional right to use a resort unit for a specified period each year.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Relative sales value method</div><div class="glossary-def">A cost allocation method assigning shared resort development costs to individual intervals in proportion to their estimated relative sales values.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc978 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
